#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166592 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166754 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e25ae0e-d10c-5fa4-924e-4c6a7efebac6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Inverse Sinh Function"}] :question/id #uuid "181b2a5e-7caf-5527-8602-e67a945affde" :question/math-academy-id "e-8337" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nIf $y = 2\\arsinh{x^2},$ then find $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic sine function is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\arsinh x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2+1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{({x}^{2})}^{2}+1}}\\cdot {({x}^{2})}^{′} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{4}+1}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{4x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{4}+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166593 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166755 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eaab6e41-d34d-5eca-a41f-5ebe58a6b178" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Inverse Cosh Function"}] :question/id #uuid "ce19d882-3c04-5ef1-8393-7de582df188d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8338" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nIf $y =\\arcosh(x+1),$ then find $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosine function is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\arcosh x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2-1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(x+1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(x+1)^{2}-1}}\\cdot (x+1)^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+2x+1)-1}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x(x+2)}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166594 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166756 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1979b91d-2ac0-5d35-b4cc-1c9c9505b503" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Inverse Tanh Function"}] :question/id #uuid "d5eeb5a1-4bfd-5f1e-b261-548c91eec64b" :question/math-academy-id "e-8339" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nIf $y = \\artanh{4x},$ then find $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic tangent function is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\artanh x) = \\dfrac{1}{1-x^2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡4x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-{(4x)}^{2}}\\cdot {(4x)}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-16{x}^{2}}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{1-16{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166595 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166757 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57d851ce-44b5-5713-ba65-7f5a9cc1122f" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Inverse Hyperbolic Functions Using the Product and Quotient Rules"}] :question/id #uuid "7cd3958b-aa1c-5c9a-b2ef-a21002aa7ff1" :question/math-academy-id "e-8340" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nIf $w(x) = e^{2x}\\artanh{x},$ then $w'(x)=2e^{2x}\\artanh{x} + y(x).$ What is $y(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nHere, we have the product of two functions $u(x) = e^{2x}$ and $v(x)=\\artanh{x}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}^{′}(x) & ={({e}^{2x})}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{artanh}⁡x+{e}^{2x}\\cdot {(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}\\cdot \\operatorname{artanh}⁡x+{e}^{2x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x+{{\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{1-{x}^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{y(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{2x}}{1 - x^2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167333 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166754 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e25ae0e-d10c-5fa4-924e-4c6a7efebac6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Inverse Sinh Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0f0a6cc-f3e3-569e-b211-f208ca96ace6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{4x^{2} - 1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165208} {:answer/id #uuid "c65e44a3-9666-5646-8b2e-2e9cf2e061fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{1 - 4x^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165209} {:answer/id #uuid "1f677e14-e33e-5a80-99c1-2a74e297ef9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{1 - 4x^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165210} {:answer/id #uuid "40bd90ba-c494-5b2a-a445-008704d533fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{4x^{2} + 1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165211} {:answer/id #uuid "5f3657a0-a096-52aa-b381-3d79f55272d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{4x^{2} + 1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165212}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2d14d448-a840-5321-9b6a-51cdd2f8b8d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165207}] :question/id #uuid "d81cff16-2de4-5af9-90cd-bc5738649620" :question/math-academy-id "q-95242" :question/problem "If $y = 3arsinh 2x$, then $\\frac{dy}{dx} =$"}]}
